{"name":"Water Carrier","hex":"#4999a1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Water Carrier","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The constellation and zodiacal sign Aquarius."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Water_Carrier"}],"entries":[{"title":"Water carrier","blocks":[{"text":"Water carrier is a profession that existed before the advent of centralized water supply systems. A water carrier collected water from a source and transported or carried containers with water to people's homes. After the construction of pipe networks, the profession of water carrier became unnecessary and disappeared."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Jean-Baptiste_Vanmour_%281671-1737%29_%28after%29_-_Turkish_Men_and_Women%2C_%27Derviche_qui_donne_l%E2%80%99eau_par_charit%C3%A9%27_-_803040.14.3_-_National_Trust.jpg/330px-Jean-Baptiste_Vanmour_%281671-1737%29_%28after%29_-_Turkish_Men_and_Women%2C_%27Derviche_qui_donne_l%E2%80%99eau_par_charit%C3%A9%27_-_803040.14.3_-_National_Trust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_carrier","wikidata":"Q965417"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water","wikidata":"Q283"},{"title":"Color of water","blocks":[{"text":"The color of water varies with the ambient conditions in which that water is present. While relatively small quantities of water appear to be colorless and transparent, pure water has a slight blue color that becomes deeper as the thickness of the observed sample increases. The hue of water is an intrinsic property and is caused by selective absorption and scattering of blue light. Dissolved elements or suspended impurities may give water a different color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG/330px-Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_of_water","wikidata":"Q5148636"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Le_Kef_les_porteurs_d%27eau.JPG/120px-Le_Kef_les_porteurs_d%27eau.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Le_Kef_les_porteurs_d%27eau.JPG/250px-Le_Kef_les_porteurs_d%27eau.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Le_Kef_les_porteurs_d%27eau.JPG/120px-Le_Kef_les_porteurs_d%27eau.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALe_Kef_les_porteurs_d'eau.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Weeks_Edwin_Water_Carriers_Of_The_Ganges.jpg/120px-Weeks_Edwin_Water_Carriers_Of_The_Ganges.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thum